ed bean’s weight, but it’s potent at parts per million. You can extract that flavor into milk and cream without transferring all the dark solids. The dark specks are a visual signature that the product contains real ground vanilla seeds, but they aren’t the main carriers of flavor — they’re just the most visible evidence. So even when a product screams “full of real vanilla!” from the speck density, the actual weight percentage of vanilla materials is still far below 1% of the total recipe. **4. Companies aren’t using “a ton” — they’re using an ounce or two per gallon.** A typical commercial premium vanilla ice cream might use around 0.2% vanilla extract by weight (or an equivalent amount of bean seeds). That’s about 1–2 ounces of extract per gallon, or a few split beans per gallon for super-premium. In volume terms, that’s a splash in a sea of milk and cream. In cost terms, however, that tiny splash can represent 30–50% of the raw ingredient cost for that flavor. So the price tag reflects the scarcity of the material, not the physical bulk of it in your bowl. **5. The “white background” rule still holds, no matter how pricey the beans.** Even if you used an entire bean’s worth of seeds per scoop (which would be astronomically expensive and probably gritty), the seeds would still be suspended as black specks in a white fat/protein emulsion. The liquid part doesn’t turn dark. You’d have a very expensive, speck-laden white ice cream. So in short: “second most expensive spice” means that the **small amount** you do use carries a disproportionately large price tag, making the final product costly. It doesn’t mean the product contains a large volume of vanilla. The specks are a crafty visual cue that screams authenticity, but they’re lightweight and leave the ice cream white, even if you can afford a blizzard of them.
